Monday's horrific mass shooting at the  DC Navy Yard left 12 victims dead and  more than a dozen people wounded it's  raised impassioned understandable but  ultimately misguided calls for more gun  control the fact is legislation should  almost never be crafted in the heat of a  crisis whether it's truly awful drug  laws pushed in the wake of high-profile  celebrity deaths or national security  measures rushed on read through Congress  after the 911 attacks legislation should  be the product of serious and  dispassionate deliberation northeastern  university's James Allen Fox has firmly  established that there is no upper trend  of mass shootings over the past 40 years  and when it comes to violent crime the  trend is clearly down FBI statistics  show that murders committed with  firearms dropped between two thousand  eight and two thousand twelve over the  past decade rape sexual assault robbery  and aggravated assault committed with  weapons has declined by twenty six  percent overall violent crime rates are  about half of what they were twenty  years ago the Navy Yard gunman Aaron  Alexis somehow managed to get a security  clearance to work at a military  installation despite getting booted from  the Navy and having a history of  gun-related violence mental instability  and erratic behavior the Pentagon is  auditing its security clearance and  hiring process for its bases one assumes  that other public and private workplaces  will follow suit of course that comes  too late to offer any comfort to the  families friends and coworkers mourning  the dead and wounded but in the end it's  far more likely to be effective than any  sort of gun control legislation rush  through Congress  you
 

